{"id": "P00377", "name": "Henry Kravis", "name_chinese": "亨利·克拉维斯", "country": "United States", "net_worth_usd_m": "12814.5", "net_worth_source": "Forbes real-time", "source_of_wealth": "Private equity", "industry": "finance & investments", "sec_insider_companies": "BridgeBio Pharma, Inc. (BBIO) - TenPercentOwner; NIQ Global Intelligence plc (NIQ) - TenPercentOwner; Crescent Energy Co (CRGY) - TenPercentOwner; BrightView Holdings, Inc. (BV) - TenPercentOwner; KKR Real Estate Select Trust Inc. (NONE) - TenPercentOwner", "shared_with": "", "appears_on": "Forbes real-time (2026); Forbes annual (2001-2025); Hurun Global (2014-2026); Wikidata (2025); SEC insider holdings (2026)", "focus": ["energy"], "forbes_profile_url": "https://www.forbes.com/profile/henry-kravis/", "wikidata_url": "http://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q933721", "forbes_bio": ["Henry Kravis cofounded KKR with his cousin, George Roberts, and Jerome Kohlberg (d. 2015) in 1976.", "Kravis and Roberts gave up their co-CEO titles in 2021; both now serve as executive co-chairs. Kohlberg left the firm in 1987.", "The duo took KKR public on the New York Stock Exchange in 2010 and converted it into a corporation in 2018.", "KKR has some more than 250 portfolio companies and more than $650 billion in assets under management, making it one of the five largest private equity firms.", "Kravis donated $100 million to Columbia University Business School in 2010 and another $25 million in 2015."], "forbes_facts": ["His economist wife, Marie-Josée Kravis, is president of the board of trustees at New York's Museum of Modern Art.", "In 1989, KKR orchestrated the $25 billion buyout of RJR Nabisco; the deal became the basis of the bestselling book \"Barbarians at the Gate.\""]}
